WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:05.790
For every sequel that successfully clawed
its way into movie theaters, there's an unmade

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:09.350
take that wowed on paper but floundered in
development.

00:00:09.350 --> 00:00:14.000
The Predator franchise has seen more than
a few proposed sequels hit the cutting room

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:16.750
floor, and we break them down right here.

00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:21.500
The concept of pitting Xenomorphs against
Predators first appeared in the anthology

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:24.470
comic book series Dark Horse Presents in 1989.

00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:30.260
A year later, Predator 2 further indulged
a showdown with an easter egg of a Xenomorph

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:32.750
skull in a Predator trophy case.

00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:37.829
But the "Alien versus Predator" concept didn't
make earnest moves toward the big screen until

00:00:37.829 --> 00:00:43.050
1991, when future Hellboy co-writer Peter
Briggs penned a spec script.

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:48.040
Briggs' initial draft, titled The Hunt: Alien
vs Predator, adheres closely to the original

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:50.950
Dark Horse comic penned by Randy Stradley.

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:55.140
Taking place off-world and in the future,
Briggs' script focuses on a clan of Predators

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:59.469
who use planets seeded with Xenomorph eggs
as hunting grounds.

00:00:59.469 --> 00:01:04.739
As a challenge, their leader includes a queen
facehugger in the batch of eggs unleashed

00:01:04.739 --> 00:01:06.219
on a jungle planet.

00:01:06.219 --> 00:01:08.850
As you'd imagine, this does not go well.

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:14.630
In 1992, Aliens magazine reported that Roland
Emmerich was attached to direct, though nothing

00:01:14.630 --> 00:01:15.639
came of it.

00:01:15.639 --> 00:01:20.609
Emmerich and his creative partner Dean Devlin
were especially keen on the idea of the Predator-Alien

00:01:20.609 --> 00:01:23.329
hybrid only hinted at in Briggs' draft.

00:01:23.329 --> 00:01:29.329
Fox effectively shelved the project in 1993
after producer Joe Roth left the studio.

00:01:29.329 --> 00:01:35.439
In 2002, producer John Davis rejected a treatment
penned by James DeMonaco and Kevin Fox on

00:01:35.439 --> 00:01:40.350
the basis that he wanted to set the film on
Earth and move away from the Dark Horse comics.

00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:47.340
At the end of Predator 2, an elder Predator
rewards Mike Harrigan with a trophy.

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:50.109
"Take it."

00:01:50.109 --> 00:01:56.490
The gift is an antique pistol engraved with
the inscription "Raphael Adolini 1715."

00:01:56.490 --> 00:02:00.240
It's a memorable moment that hints at just
how long the Predators have been terrorizing

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:01.630
our planet.

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:06.429
It also raises a whole swath of questions
that, it turns out, were intended to find

00:02:06.429 --> 00:02:09.979
answers in a sequel that never materialized.

00:02:09.979 --> 00:02:14.840
As director/writer duo Jim and John Thomas
note in the Predator 2 director's commentary,

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:19.319
they were constantly toying around with the
idea of a Predator film set on Earth in a

00:02:19.319 --> 00:02:21.849
time before modern weapons.

00:02:21.849 --> 00:02:25.189
The brothers have said:
"We played around on the set [with] doing

00:02:25.189 --> 00:02:26.469
something historical.

00:02:26.469 --> 00:02:30.780
Like, in a time where you had nothing but
steam engines and flintlocks and you were

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:33.140
up against something like [the Predator].

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:34.239
That could be fun."

00:02:34.239 --> 00:02:38.959
The brothers' brainstorming sessions never
materialized into anything concrete.

00:02:38.959 --> 00:02:43.099
And yet, while we were robbed of a big-screen
Predator period piece, that didn't stop Dark

00:02:43.099 --> 00:02:48.430
Horse Comics from picking up the Thomas brothers'
enticing engraved pistol and running with

00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:49.430
it.

00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:54.400
Published in July 1996 and written by Henry
Gilroy, Predator: 1718 tells the story of

00:02:54.400 --> 00:03:00.159
Raphael Adolini, his pistol, and how the Predators
in Predator 2 came to acquire it.

00:03:00.159 --> 00:03:04.730
Set in the Bermuda Triangle, the comic paints
Adolini as a pirate captain suddenly faced

00:03:04.730 --> 00:03:08.019
with a rebellious crew and an unlikely alien
ally.

00:03:09.019 --> 00:03:13.760
Before earning his first screenwriting credit
on 2010's Predators, Alex Litvak worked at

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:15.720
20th Century Fox in the 1990s.

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:20.240
He’s talked about how the studio's attitude
at the time was that you couldn't make another

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:23.920
Predator movie without the original film's
star: Arnold Schwarzenegger.

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:28.650
Responding to this limitation, Litvak pitched
a sequel that envisioned the triumphant return

00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:31.609
of Schwarzenegger's Major Alan "Dutch" Schaefer.

00:03:31.609 --> 00:03:36.150
Litvak's pitch took place directly after the
events of the original Predator.

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:40.599
As the script tells it, the whole affair from
the first film has been classified and Dutch

00:03:40.599 --> 00:03:43.010
is living out his days in exile.

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:47.480
Then, a Predator ship crashes at a remote
location and the government taps Dutch to

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:51.120
lead a spry team of commandos to investigate.

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:55.870
When they finally reach the ship, a group
of Predators emerge with one target: Dutch.

00:03:55.870 --> 00:03:57.439
He's the one that got away.

00:03:57.439 --> 00:03:59.909
And they've come back to claim their prize.

00:03:59.909 --> 00:04:05.029
As Litvak explains, the pitch was a pointed
attempt to seduce Schwarzenegger.

00:04:05.029 --> 00:04:08.900
Whether Litvak's proposed script even made
it to the actor is unclear.

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:14.120
In any case, the studio resolved that Schwarzenegger
was still off the table and the pitch never

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:15.409
saw the light of day.

00:04:16.409 --> 00:04:20.840
While waiting to work on Desperado in the
early 1990s, Robert Rodriguez wrote a script

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:22.419
for a third Predator film.

00:04:22.419 --> 00:04:27.800
An unconfirmed copy of his script has since
made the rounds on the internet, and it is,

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:31.060
to put it mildly, completely bananas.

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:35.470
The story begins on an alien planet, where
a group of humans outfitted in Predator armor

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:37.490
attack a Spanish galleon.

00:04:37.490 --> 00:04:41.780
The cloaked attackers overwhelm the ship and
transport their captive, Dutch from the first

00:04:41.780 --> 00:04:43.910
film, to a prison planet.

00:04:43.910 --> 00:04:49.520
The planet also happens to be a gladiatorial
training ground for "teen" Predators, replete

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:52.849
with, among other things, human-alien hybrids.

00:04:52.849 --> 00:04:57.310
Rodriguez approached Arnold Schwarzenegger
about returning to his original role as Dutch.

00:04:57.310 --> 00:05:01.500
Supposedly there was even a role in the script
written for Jean-Claude Van Damme.

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:05.860
Rodriguez's script would ultimately morph
into 2010's Predators, thanks to an extensive

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:08.780
overhaul by Alex Litvak and Michael Finch.

00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:14.110
However, at the time, Fox passed on the script
because Rodriguez's sequel was simply too

00:05:14.110 --> 00:05:15.460
ambitious and expensive.

00:05:16.460 --> 00:05:22.130
While working on 2007's Aliens vs. Predator:
Requiem as a visual effects consultant, Liam

00:05:22.130 --> 00:05:24.900
O'Donnell drafted a sequel treatment.

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:30.260
O'Donnell's idea for Aliens vs. Predator 3
took place on a future Earth ravaged by climate

00:05:30.260 --> 00:05:35.539
change where mankind has reverse-engineered
advanced interstellar travel with technology

00:05:35.539 --> 00:05:37.389
recovered from Predator weapons.

00:05:37.389 --> 00:05:42.419
However, the fuel that powers this spacefaring
tech is derived from a rare mineral only found

00:05:42.419 --> 00:05:47.870
in southern Africa, the last continent free
from the vice grip of the Weyland-Yutani Corporation.

00:05:47.870 --> 00:05:52.340
The warming of the planet causes the release
of the original Alien vs. Predator queen from

00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:53.960
her icy tomb.

00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:59.020
And the film ends with a third act three-way
fight between a Predator, Alien, and human.

00:05:59.020 --> 00:06:01.639
The treatment was ultimately scrapped for
two reasons.

00:06:01.639 --> 00:06:06.631
First, as O'Donnell puts it: After the addition
of the Predator home planet to Requiem, there

00:06:06.631 --> 00:06:10.759
was an assumption that if they did a third
movie, it had to be in space.

00:06:10.759 --> 00:06:16.250
Second: Upon its release, Aliens vs. Predator:
Requiem failed miserably at the box office,

00:06:16.250 --> 00:06:18.199
basically scrapping any hope for a sequel.

00:06:19.319 --> 00:06:25.319
In 2015, Vincenzo Natali tweeted a slew of
concept art for, among other things, a tentative

00:06:25.319 --> 00:06:26.590
Predator reboot.

00:06:26.590 --> 00:06:31.400
The visual pitch offers a brief glimpse into
what we could have seen: An expressionistic

00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:36.630
jungle war zone, a sleek and spiky Predator,
and a brutal gladiatorial showdown.

00:06:36.630 --> 00:06:42.120
Natali pitched his take on what would ultimately
become Predators with nothing but a logline:

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:44.479
"Predators hunt humans on their planet."

00:06:44.479 --> 00:06:49.280
Natali has elaborated on his visual treatment:
"When I was called in to pitch, there was

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:50.280
nothing.

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:51.280
There was no script.

00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:54.870
There was only the notion that this was going
to take place on a Predator planet.

00:06:54.870 --> 00:06:59.520
I think there was a Dangerous Game aspect
to it with humans being hunted by Predators.

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:05.050
I met with Robert Rodriguez and some executives,
and I just did a whole bunch of drawings."

00:07:05.050 --> 00:07:07.530
Nothing ever came from that bunch of drawings.

00:07:07.530 --> 00:07:11.939
And that's a shame, because the idea of the
director of Cube and Splice taking a stab

00:07:11.939 --> 00:07:15.940
at a Predator movie sounds like a match made
in sci-fi horror heaven.

00:07:16.940 --> 00:07:22.000
In 2010, director Nimród Antal and producer
Robert Rodriguez revitalized the franchise

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:27.159
with Predators, an action-packed sequel set
on an alien planet turned hunting ground.

00:07:27.159 --> 00:07:31.530
The honor-obsessed aliens use a planetary
game preserve to test their abilities, hunting

00:07:31.530 --> 00:07:34.979
down humans as well as creatures from other
planets.

00:07:34.979 --> 00:07:37.610
By the end, only two survivors remain.

00:07:37.610 --> 00:07:41.330
As the pair catch their breath at the end
of the film, a new hunt begins, and our heroes

00:07:41.330 --> 00:07:43.590
realize that they have to escape.

00:07:43.590 --> 00:07:49.310
"Now, let's find a way off this f--king planet."

00:07:49.310 --> 00:07:53.879
Despite its obvious sequel setup, a Predators
follow-up has yet to materialize.

00:07:53.879 --> 00:07:58.910
Which makes co-writer Alex Litvak's enticing
proposal for a direct sequel an absolute knife

00:07:58.910 --> 00:08:00.020
twist.

00:08:00.020 --> 00:08:05.030
Litvak has described his pitch for Predators
2 as "Die Hard on a Predator ship."

00:08:05.030 --> 00:08:08.909
In Litvak's sequel, Royce and Isabelle let
the Predators catch them.

00:08:08.909 --> 00:08:12.870
They are then brought aboard the ship that
the aliens use to transport people to the

00:08:12.870 --> 00:08:13.870
planet.

00:08:13.870 --> 00:08:17.550
They break out of their cells and as they
attempt to seize control of the ship, Die

00:08:17.550 --> 00:08:19.300
Hard in space ensues.

00:08:19.300 --> 00:08:21.650
Eventually, our heroes make it back to Earth.

00:08:21.650 --> 00:08:26.510
And then, per Litvak:
"We land and realize it's the future!

00:08:26.510 --> 00:08:31.080
All along we thought this was happening now,
but what if these guys have been on ice for

00:08:31.080 --> 00:08:32.490
300 years?

00:08:32.490 --> 00:08:35.150
The hatch opens, and the Space Marines come
in!"

00:08:35.150 --> 00:08:39.790
This move would have once again merged the
Alien and Predator franchises under one roof.

00:08:39.790 --> 00:08:44.361
As Litvak tells it, although 20th Century
Fox was enthusiastic, his proposed sequel

00:08:44.361 --> 00:08:46.330
never got off the ground.

